## Problem

The v0.4 evaluator is a useful deterministic scorer, but it is not yet a scientific evaluation system. `src/rob2_kit/evaluation/harness.py` scores records supplied in a synthetic fixture; retrieval is represented only as `retrieved_passages` grouped by Trial, and the shipped fixture is intentionally incomplete. It cannot distinguish evidence present in the projection, retrieved into a candidate cache, returned by the server, delivered by a host, inspected, cited, used for the right premise, or translated into the right signaling answer.

That makes the final label too late to diagnose. The current 10-Trial reference run at `51c281b2b4a6` produced provisional agreement of 33/50 Domains (66%), 2/10 overall judgments (20%), and 1/10 complete five-Domain vectors (10%). An always-Low comparator was 86% for Domains and 40% for overall/full-vector agreement. Those figures come from one draw and provisional references; they are development evidence, not validated clinical accuracy or proof that any proposed intervention works.

This is a successor to closed #273. That issue delivered the scorer skeleton and synthetic coverage checks. The remaining gap is expert reference construction, observable trajectory collection, leakage-safe splits, and paired evaluation of real workflows.

## Outcome

Build a privacy-safe, provider-independent development and holdout evaluation keyed by Trial, approved Result, Domain, signaling question, draw, model family/version, kit revision, and intervention. Expert references should record, where feasible:

- approved target/Result identity and adjudication status;
- required passages and acceptable alternative passage sets, scoped by question and Source role;
- required scientific premises, explicit unknowns, and material contradictions;
- signaling answers, Domain judgments, overall judgment, and documented legitimate ambiguity.

Instrument a diagnostic evidence-to-judgment funnel:

1. source projection fidelity;
2. candidates retrieved into the server-side ranking;
3. passages surfaced in a server response;
4. content actually delivered by the host, only where observable;
5. passages inspected through any valid route;
6. Evidence cited/selected;
7. required premise supported and contradiction handled;
8. signaling answer and derived Domain/overall judgment.

These are diagnostic states, not a mandatory tool sequence. A sufficient preview may be cited without `read_pages`, and a passage may arrive through prior reads or Domain context. Server-produced output is not evidence of host delivery, attention, comprehension, or use; unknown stages remain unknown.

## Experimental design

Use the current provisional 10-Trial set only for development and diagnosis. Construct an untouched holdout split by Trial and document family so reports, supplements, protocols, outcomes, and draws from one Trial cannot cross splits. Stratify scientific classes for diagnostic power while also reporting performance at natural prevalence.

Run paired, one-change ablations across repeated draws and at least two model families. Freeze source bytes, projections, approved Result mapping, tool/skill contract, model/version, budget, scorer, and intervention config for each comparison. Preserve failed and incomplete runs in coverage and cost denominators. Keep repeated development experiments separate from the repository's one-attempt release qualification.

Report at least:

- needed-passage recall/rank at useful depths, Source-role coverage, and passage-alternative coverage;
- required-premise support, unsupported-premise rate, contradiction handling, and absence-search adequacy;
- signaling-answer, Domain, overall, and full-vector agreement, confusion matrices, class-balanced performance, and same-cell always-Low/majority comparators;
